**Jagged Arrays:**

A **jagged array** is an array of arrays where **each row can have different length**.

**Regular 2D vs Jagged:**

```java
// Regular 2D - All rows same length
int[][] regular = new int[3][4];  // 3 rows, each has 4 columns

// Jagged - Different row lengths
int[][] jagged = new int[3][];  // 3 rows, columns vary
jagged[0] = new int[2];         // Row 0: 2 columns
jagged[1] = new int[4];         // Row 1: 4 columns
jagged[2] = new int[3];         // Row 2: 3 columns
```

**Declaration and Initialization:**

**Method 1: Direct Initialization**
```java
int[][] jagged = {
    {1, 2},
    {3, 4, 5, 6},
    {7, 8, 9}
};
```

**Method 2: Step by Step**
```java
int[][] jagged = new int[3][];  // Only rows specified
jagged[0] = new int[2];         // Set row 0 length
jagged[1] = new int[4];         // Set row 1 length
jagged[2] = new int[3];         // Set row 2 length

// Fill values
jagged[0][0] = 1;
jagged[0][1] = 2;
// ...
```

**Visual Representation:**
```
Jagged Array:
Row 0: [1][2]
Row 1: [3][4][5][6]
Row 2: [7][8][9]

vs Regular 2D:
Row 0: [1][2][3][4]
Row 1: [5][6][7][8]
Row 2: [9][10][11][12]
```

**Traversing Jagged Arrays:**
```java
// Must use row-specific length
for (int i = 0; i < jagged.length; i++) {
    System.out.print("Row " + i + ": ");
    for (int j = 0; j < jagged[i].length; j++) {  // jagged[i].length!
        System.out.print(jagged[i][j] + " ");
    }
    System.out.println();
}
```

**Use Cases:**

**1. Variable-Length Data:**
```java
// Student grades - different subjects per student
int[][] studentGrades = {
    {85, 90, 78},           // Student 1: 3 subjects
    {92, 88, 95, 87},       // Student 2: 4 subjects
    {76, 82}                // Student 3: 2 subjects
};

for (int i = 0; i < studentGrades.length; i++) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (int grade : studentGrades[i]) {
        sum += grade;
    }
    double avg = (double) sum / studentGrades[i].length;
    System.out.println("Student " + (i+1) + " avg: " + avg);
}
```

**2. Triangular Patterns:**
```java
// Pascal's triangle
int[][] pascal = new int[5][];
for (int i = 0; i < pascal.length; i++) {
    pascal[i] = new int[i + 1];  // Row i has i+1 elements
    // Fill Pascal's triangle values...
}
```

**Key Difference:**
- Regular 2D: Fixed columns per row
- Jagged: Variable columns per row
- Saves memory when rows have different sizes

